# Taste engine

Final numeric taste scores are computed in this folder. Do not generate a full dish vector with an LLM. Missing / to-taste amounts are filled *before* mix by Gemini `estimateMissingAmounts` (sibling recipes, or dish + cuisine + the rest of that recipe when every source omitted it). Code converts those amounts; mix stays deterministic.

- Combine with `combineRecipeTaste` / `attributeRecipeTaste`: recipe-relative loudness (smooth blend from `score × (score/peak)^⅓` to full score as max(score/peak, score/avg) → 1; no 85% cliff), **per-ingredient** blend of linear `share×loud` and punch `loud×share^(1/p)` (p=4; punch weight = share smoothstep midpoint ≈1.5% × loudness ~4→7 × intensity ~5.25→10). Crowding on a dimension does not change an ingredient’s punch term. Linear gain (1.75×). All six dimensions use the same path. Cap each dimension at the max in-ingredient, then clamp dish display to 0–10. Contribution tips use each ingredient’s blended points (every positive contributor, rounded to 2 decimals with a 0.01 floor; UI shows 5 then “Show more”).
- Unknown ingredients: cache → chemistry leaf (FAO/INFOODS first, then UmamiDB, Phenol-Explorer, Dr. Duke, FooDB, USDA) → Gemini `estimateLeafTaste` if no lab match for that exact name (`source: "llm"`) → nested full recipe search. One Gemini `confirmFoodShortlists` call must accept each source title against the dish context. Match the food, not a keyword collision. A branded USDA product is allowed only when it *is* that ingredient. Juice/paste/oil inherit the flavoring food, not the carrier. UmamiDB wins free glutamate/nucleotides; Phenol-Explorer wins polyphenol bitter; Dr. Duke wins pungents (ppm midpoint). FAO then USDA then FooDB fill remaining classes. Ignore potassium, vitamin C, and protein amino acids. Nutrient sodium is a salty proxy, not culinary salt — Gemini may lower salty when a mouthful is not salt-tasting. Acid-process foods (fermented/pickled/yogurt/vinegar-class) need organic-acid evidence; sodium-only rows are incomplete and fall through to estimate. Do not collapse names (thai chili ≠ chili; soft shell crab ≠ crab). Catalog mouthful scale is usually 0–10; **table salt leaf ≈ 12**. Tiny compound amounts stay tiny scores. Gemini may calibrate dimensions that have evidence, may add sour/umami when labs omit acids/free glutamate, and may add chili heat when the named food is a chili (USDA often matches sweet/bell pepper). Leaf calibrate/estimate see the parent dish and known catalog anchors (`leafCatalogAnchors`: sugar, brown sugar, mirin, soy, salt, cheese, garlic, ginger, …); they may carefully raise or lower so relative scale stays sane (mirin ≈ 5–7 sweet, never ≥ crystalline sugar; plain cheese ≈ 5 salty, not sauce-level). Do not invent chili heat for ginger, garlic, mustard, or Sichuan peppercorn (0 spicy). Lemon/lime fruit ≈ 9 sour, juice ≈ 9.5; lactic-fermented vegetables ≈ 7–8 sour; orange ≈ 7–8 sweet, onion ≈ 0–1 sweet, black pepper ≈ 0.2 spicy (freshly cracked ≈ 0.5), cheese ≈ 5 salty, parmesan ≈ 7–8 salty, soy/fish sauce ≈ 8.5–10 salty; bland vegetables stay near 0. A usual 10 is the most intense culinary form (sugar, Thai chili); salt is ≈12. Juice/paste/extract score higher than the whole food. Spicy is chili heat (Thai chili = 10). Chili in a spicy dish is the hot pepper, not sweet chili sauce.
- Representative recipe: every `in` ingredient; volume = mean share across recipes (0 if absent) so occurrence dilutes rare/substitute lines. Extract tags `in` vs `out`; only `in` scores. `mix.intensity` is contribution to the final dish; 0 (drained frying oil, pasta water, evaporated/absorbed cooking water) is dropped from tasting volume; eaten oil stays 1. Non-default intensity also gets `mix.why` (1–2 words); the ingredient hover shows `contributes: N% · why` only then. Out-only sides get an “Often served with” list under the scores (primary flavors), still listed quieter in Ingredients. Omitted amounts are filled by Gemini before this mix (`fill-amounts.ts`). Whole-food piece sizes apply only when that food is what is counted (or a cut of it); `fish sauce` / `kaffir lime leaf` / `chicken stock` do not inherit fillet/fruit/bird sizes.
- Search recipes in the origin culture’s language by default for the form `identifyDish` resolved (bare name → popular restaurant/tourist form people usually mean; specific qualifier → that variant). Typed-language mode searches in the user’s query language instead; both modes share one dish cache row.
- A recipe counts only if the live page fetch returned 2xx. Accept a recipe only when the live URL and/or fetched HTML title (`og:title` / `<title>` / `h1`) match the dish; LLM extract titles veto other dishes but must not authorize. Captcha/empty shells may fall back to the search-hit title. Multi-word Latin names need a contiguous phrase. URL Context may parse a JS shell after a 2xx fetch; 4xx/5xx pages are skipped. Captcha/human-check redirects still count as 2xx; URL Context and stored links use the pre-challenge recipe URL, not the challenge page.
- Shared dish profiles live in Turso `dishes` (running mean, `timesTasted`, last snapshot). LLM matches the query to a row; it does not invent scores. Nested recipe tastes persist as ingredients, not dishes.
- Site-wide taste count lives in Turso `stats` (`taste_count`). Increment only on a successful top-level profile, not on reject/error/Stop.
- Translate extracted ingredient names to English (or a well-known romanized native name when English is awkward) before listing or resolving. Gemini canonicalizes every name against the ingredient catalog (`canonicalizeIngredientNames(names, catalog, culinaryContext)`). Ambiguous generics must become the cuisine-typical grocery even when the catalog only has the dictionary form (Chinese dish + sausage / 香肠 → chinese sausage or lap cheong, not plain sausage); already-specific names stay themselves.
- Classify the query first (dish / ingredient / reject). Unmatched trailing junk (`]`, `!!!`) is stripped first; the dish is not renamed. If the name is both grocery and dish (lasagna, spaghetti), classify as dish and run the recipe path. Ingredient Turso wins when classify says ingredient. Reuse cache only gates top-level dishes. Brands and gibberish are rejected.
- Resolve every unique listed ingredient before scoring the representative mix.
- Default model: `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`. Hard cases: `gemini-3.6-flash`. IDs in `lib/engine/models.ts`.
- Add measured profiles in Turso (`INSERT OR IGNORE` on normalized name). Existing catalog rows are never overwritten. Unit tests may use `lib/engine/testdata/ingredients.json`; that file is not the live catalog.
- Tests first: `lib/engine/*.test.ts`.
